update lr_explanation_choice_notes t set body=v.nb from (values
('902c891c-a96b-4bce-93cd-f97e934d9689'::uuid,'a8db949b5b66474bec6abb818245acb7','The rules only require that the city invest in computer modeling technology. Buying a competing package is still investing in computer modeling technology, so the requirement would be met. And meeting a requirement doesn''t guarantee anything, so we can''t say the predicament would have stayed unresolved.'),
('5880ae4a-e75e-4d9e-87a7-1b8d3b654680'::uuid,'a1bcd3c5abbaecac54e97f363ee2e07b','This is the chain with the middle link removed. No investment means no rush-hour increase (sentence one), and no increase means the predicament isn''t resolved (sentence two). So no investment means no resolution.

The move to repeat: when two conditionals share a term, line them up so the shared term is the result of one and the trigger of the other, then connect the outer ends. Check the direction too. B keeps the "not" on both sides in the same order the rules give them, so it follows.'),
('857f0842-9bec-472d-a187-7738d81adba7'::uuid,'47632ed0a408edb695cafb503a0052f6','The stimulus only tells us about rush-hour flow. Daily totals could still fall if off-peak traffic dropped. It also never compares this year with last year. Rush-hour flow went up, but we aren''t told when, so the year-over-year comparison has no support.'),
('43195df6-1964-4a96-95fe-fca99bdca1d3'::uuid,'77be3f9d2a1846c999c7d30551765ca3','The requirement was the investment, made "at the request of the city''s mayor." Nothing says the mayor made it "the highest budgetary priority." The city could have invested as a modest line item and the requirement would still have been met, so nothing shows the top-priority ranking was needed for the increase.'),
('07175d96-eacd-448c-ba5b-1efeca7a15fc'::uuid,'d460657571ddcc944afc015195c62f70','We know the mayor requested the investment, but we''re never told why. The mayor might have wanted it for something else entirely, and the traffic gain could have been a side effect. A motive is a new claim, and nothing in the rules supports it.'),
('1ba9f93a-e161-4092-aa9b-4847ae6d45ae'::uuid,'5ca566f718e8a12e32cf6d53698155fe','This compares sewage pollution with pollution "in some other way." The stimulus never mentions any other kind of pollution, so we have nothing to compare against. Even for sewage, the fish kill only happens "sometimes," so we can''t rank sewage as the deadlier pollutant.'),
('60b97f01-e22f-43fd-aa71-03aeb36a0cf0'::uuid,'f9f2c654ff722d95d0435bb5fac5d316','The stimulus says the algae give toxic microorganisms "a rich food source." It says nothing about how fast those microorganisms reproduce or how they compare with other microorganisms. Plenty of food for one group doesn''t prove that group outgrows all the others.'),
('2fda1789-c0e0-44df-85a1-6f053ce8a0b2'::uuid,'11613dd335479a4f1bb0769289ec920f','The stimulus describes one way sewage can harm fish: the algae it produces feed microorganisms that are toxic to fish. It never rules out other kinds of harm, so saying sewage does not harm fish "in any way other than" this one goes too far.'),
('d5985aac-6834-4aa5-b3a2-6293ba6f8ccb'::uuid,'b4fa96a7e9315d17b8f351cb90732bd5','Sewage pollution is one way to get algae to proliferate. Nothing says it''s the only way. D treats a cause as a requirement. Test it: an estuary with no sewage that gets nutrients from farm runoff could still fill with algae, and nothing in the stimulus says otherwise.'),
('702c16eb-6f6d-4fcd-8897-8b6b1ade5c68'::uuid,'f8d21f356695cfbad5ceb726ca68b9a9','This connects the first cause to the final effect: sewage overnutrifies the water, the water grows algae, and the algae sometimes feed microorganisms that kill most of the fish.

The word "can" is what makes it safe. Because the last link only happens "sometimes," the chain proves possibility, not certainty. When a chain has a weak link, the right answer keeps that weakness in its wording.'),
('82131486-4dbf-4f30-8846-a80cdceb4c08'::uuid,'3b1c74e33e6b46ae4e7286e0dae03638','"Most nurseries" is a claim about the whole industry. The stimulus only tells us about some nurseries and one variety, and we don''t even know whether that variety is mislabeled. Nothing supports a count of most.'),
('226e6c8a-a816-42e6-8ff1-fb3b19f4bb40'::uuid,'86eb6c93a43ebd8f582f0d51b977090d','B says a correct label requires the variety to be unsuitable. The rule points the other way: a correct "miniature" label requires that the variety be suitable. And if the variety is suitable, nothing stops the "miniature" labels from being correct, so correct labeling clearly doesn''t depend on unsuitability.'),
('dbbe72b4-eaf2-4170-80bb-7e74649c7d6b'::uuid,'551c792a00bfaf308c17a0b271830762','This is the tempting one. It''s true only if Stark Sweet Melody is unsuitable for a tub or pot, and the stimulus never says so. If the variety is suitable, the rule doesn''t fire and the "miniature" labels might be correct. C states as fact what E correctly keeps as a condition.'),
('1b2c4d25-47bc-4b42-ad93-fce69ac8e07b'::uuid,'94c4e80447b78f4b6fbc4c9eaf9d002c','The rule only says when a "miniature" label can''t be correct. It never says when a tree must be labeled "miniature." Suitability is a requirement, not a guarantee, so no tree is ever shown to deserve the label. We can''t prove that leaving it off is a mistake.'),
('732360e8-3302-4483-a0b2-63ef7cb97c3c'::uuid,'fc45eb614553d8cb83fa6890f4d82d13','"Unless X, Y" means "if not X, then Y." So E says: if Stark Sweet Melody is not suitable for a tub or pot, some nurseries mislabel it. That''s our prediction. An unsuitable variety can''t be correctly labeled "miniature," and some nurseries label it "miniature," so those nurseries are wrong.

The move to repeat: when the fact you need to fire a rule is missing, look for an answer that turns the missing fact into an "if" (or an "unless").'),
('2a267b7e-79bf-4109-afab-69671f0fb30b'::uuid,'234672678708de8cebde7fc505b71bef','The stimulus only tells individuals what they "should not do." It never says what a society should require. And the harm comes from public inaction being emulated, so a celebrity who quietly skips voting threatens nothing. Nothing supports forcing every celebrity to vote.'),
('c16e0540-7c3f-4ac5-9f4e-1b22a56d7d3a'::uuid,'ec884145d1587aed60ed21553fb8cad0','This makes voting itself need a justification. The stimulus never says voting causes harm, so the first principle gives no reason to hold back from voting. Worse, under B an ordinary person whose vote won''t inspire anyone has no reason to vote, and many people reasoning that way is exactly the harm the stimulus warns about.'),
('8b264e58-f23b-4436-8782-ce5d05e19f34'::uuid,'40bc485fa062dc5410bf7393fd8a5fea','The stimulus says respected people are emulated. It never says who deserves respect. Whether non-voting celebrities should lose respect is a new judgment, and none of the statements has the power to prove it.'),
('28612e4c-1a0b-4863-8afb-936c118fe6cf'::uuid,'fbfff5c43cad8155f7840000119097b9','The stimulus describes who gets emulated; it never tells anyone whom to emulate. It also says nothing about celebrities who are not widely respected. D mixes the stimulus''s words into an instruction to a different audience about a group the stimulus never discusses.'),
('b930fb56-7464-4012-bca2-345e066469b7'::uuid,'6ac74f6393a4118c519ed8d1aecf173e','This is the chain''s end. Widely respected people''s public inactions are emulated, so if they publicly don''t vote, many may follow. Many people not voting brings serious harm, and one should not do anything with that potential.

E also gets the scope right. It doesn''t say anyone must vote, only that they shouldn''t refrain publicly, which is exactly what the emulation statement makes risky. On a principle-inference question, the right answer''s "should" usually comes straight from a "should" in the stimulus, applied to the case the other statements set up.'),
('d47c8c6b-221a-4a00-9d9f-c447fe693dd8'::uuid,'4c1ce9b7480f20cc9b64c4757503dfd6','The rule is upper-level → prerequisite. A reads it as if only upper-level courses can have prerequisites. With no new upper-level courses, the only possible new course is Dean Wilson''s, and nothing says a lower-level course can''t have a prerequisite. That''s a negated conditional, and it doesn''t follow.'),
('7bd23a4a-b955-457c-90dd-70007458c0ea'::uuid,'9e959a7c47b21b38de1c276d79f63279','Nothing in the stimulus makes any proposal get approved. A proposal is a requirement for approval, not a guarantee. Test it: the committee received five proposals, two were approved, and both new courses are upper-level. B''s trigger is met and its result is false.'),
('a4e83439-c86a-406c-b25d-1dddae4c3d10'::uuid,'c6410aa008eac6599f74489150e034b1','Two or more new courses can''t all come from Dean Wilson, who has only one proposal. So at least one came from the committee. Committee proposals are all upper-level, and upper-level courses next year have prerequisites. So at least one new course has a prerequisite.

The move to repeat: when a rule offers two sources and one is capped at a single item, ask what happens once you need more than that cap. The extra items have to come from the other source, and everything you know about that source applies to them.'),
('1fd9e53a-a5df-4562-8a5d-524d1b387185'::uuid,'a3048509cd7ecf4f83f60f5b116c88f8','Even if every new course is upper-level and has a prerequisite, "all courses offered next year" includes courses that already exist. The prerequisite rule covers upper-level courses only, so existing lower-level courses could run without prerequisites.'),
('7c47b190-8749-4b29-a5f6-eb66b57784ce'::uuid,'0b1b7d4bc8d88fea3e0f710a7497c3a2','With no new upper-level courses, the committee''s proposals clearly weren''t approved. That tells us nothing about Dean Wilson''s proposal. Maybe it was approved, maybe nothing new was approved at all. Approval is never guaranteed by anything in the stimulus.'),
('359f5471-9a11-49ad-b15b-d90fb2b92f99'::uuid,'a6b1a883923227119788c407ad67b97b','Overcoming an inclination means resisting it, not losing the ability to act on it. The stimulus even says overcoming is "often difficult to do, even for a morally virtuous person," which suggests virtuous people still feel the pull. Nothing makes them "incapable" of doing wrong.'),
('42bb6b2d-beab-49fc-b9c4-76c1d00f8e19'::uuid,'69a4cad2f1ad702db871eea209f8ee85','The rule says virtue requires excellence. It doesn''t say excellence produces virtue. Plenty of people could achieve moral excellence and never become virtuous, so we can''t say how many of them are virtuous, let alone "most."'),
('3fdb242d-e172-451f-af5e-fb59d81aec5d'::uuid,'4a0e323dcc61c113bc0814b7925317cd','C treats having no wrong inclinations as enough for moral excellence. The stimulus only gives a requirement for excellence: repeatedly overcoming such inclinations. A requirement never guarantees anything, and a person with nothing to overcome hasn''t shown they met even that requirement.'),
('9be247ed-fc55-4508-95d0-2bc60ce62bf3'::uuid,'eac7bf2d1e4bbd23bbe4a7b494a75b56','This is the most tempting trap. The rule is virtuous → excellence. D says not virtuous → no excellence, which negates both sides without flipping them. The valid contrapositive is no excellence → not virtuous. Excellence is required for virtue, not limited to people who are already virtuous, so a non-virtuous person achieving it is consistent with every statement.'),
('9f4ef585-edda-41b5-b5ea-61fc558935a1'::uuid,'0f3dd48fd9ff9df6759c1a9bc53b6891','Chain the two rules: virtue requires excellence, and excellence requires repeatedly overcoming inclinations to do wrong. So every virtuous person has overcome such inclinations, and you can''t overcome an inclination you never had.

The move to repeat: when two "only" rules share a term, connect the outer ends, then ask what the final requirement itself presupposes. The right answer here states that presupposition.'),
('9bf9af78-c1ff-4171-8855-60df2e39885f'::uuid,'ed5cae321750368076a01c683c2fe1b3','Nothing in the report counts against small screens. It says unlimited tiles can be joined, not that a screen needs many tiles. A compound screen of just a few bright tiles fits everything we''re told.'),
('39a9401b-5cd4-4c88-ae7f-5858da4e7447'::uuid,'101abb4c5ddd848e4dae857a69bbc618','The report is about what the technology can do, not what buyers want. Test it: every sentence could be true even if nobody wanted to buy one of these sets. Demand is a new topic.'),
('3094c624-6649-4953-a36b-6587ea8759fb'::uuid,'ed4cd6ea44cc9148d4f1d8b64b815933','The report describes size and brightness at one fixed thickness: "a set of this thickness." It never changes the thickness, so it can''t tell us what a thicker set does to brightness. C invents a second relationship from a sentence that held thickness constant.'),
('15f1e9c6-1913-4426-b843-71cb92a57ccb'::uuid,'b62d7d0ffd3a59c1b4227ec5dfccf863','This is the problem and the fix put together. Noncompound screens in thin sets get dimmer as they grow. Compound screens are thin, each tile is bright, and unlimited tiles can be added without losing brightness or adding thickness. So a thin set can now have a screen that''s both bright and very large.

The move to repeat: when a stimulus states a limitation and then says "however" and "moreover" about a new approach, the inference is usually that the new approach gets past that limitation.'),
('9071ab01-af53-4183-b3f6-ba3609cf6036'::uuid,'282ddd0fd083ad0f64e02b3497ee225f','The report lists advantages. It never says those are the only differences. Cost, resolution or durability could all be worse with compound screens, and nothing in the report rules that out. Silence about disadvantages doesn''t prove there are none.'),
('79776865-8d4b-4b95-bf94-665de1d5b8b4'::uuid,'2ec27b383d884592baecf2ab71d4a06f','The stimulus proves the opposite for some beliefs. The beliefs in the second sentence aren''t grounded in observable evidence, yet they come from a reliable source and so are reasonable. A belief can lack observable evidence and still be reasonable.'),
('5ac3e540-b9bb-4d34-920e-33fcea124682'::uuid,'94dd89528a9246a806f4bf0eab0adc71','The second sentence directly contradicts this. It describes beliefs that are based on a reliable source and are not self-evident. One counterexample is enough to sink an "all" claim, and the stimulus hands us that counterexample.'),
('6b221977-e312-4303-8fab-e3861fa7b159'::uuid,'b462f419ece11dd33b3e57b17905b5f1','The rule runs reliable source → reasonable. C runs it backward: reasonable (with no observable evidence) → reliable source. A reliable source is one way to make a belief reasonable, not the only way. A reasonable belief could rest on something else entirely.'),
('df219328-2674-438e-9f17-2b4ff1f8a6af'::uuid,'1f8554d206c3295b5f70ef4b36b260ad','This turns a "some" fact into a rule. The second sentence shows some beliefs lack both traits, but a group that lacks both doesn''t prove that lacking one forces lacking the other. Nothing rules out a belief that is self-evident yet not grounded in observable evidence.'),
('21b9fc7c-c685-4555-9802-c12811a5ff77'::uuid,'eb758e50ed392baedea71c902b2f3b42','Run the beliefs from the second sentence through the rule. They''re based on a reliable source, so they''re reasonable. They''re also not self-evident and not grounded in observable evidence. So among reasonable beliefs that aren''t self-evident, some aren''t grounded in observable evidence.

The move to repeat: when a "some" fact triggers a rule, the rule''s result attaches to that group, and you can pair it with any other trait the group has. The right answer is often a "some" statement built from those traits.')
) v(id,ob,nb) where t.id=v.id and md5(t.body)=v.ob returning t.id;